WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:02.560
Dwight Kurt Schrute has shown himself to be,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:02.560 --> 00:00:08.080
at various points, both terribly misguided and&nbsp;
an unexpected voice of reason on "The Office."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.080 --> 00:00:12.960
More often than you might expect, the eccentric&nbsp;
beet farmer and assistant to the regional manager&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:17.760
played by Rainn Wilson on the classic NBC&nbsp;
sitcom is actually worth listening to.

00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:21.520
Despite his strangeness, Dwight often&nbsp;
shows a knack for problem-solving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.520 --> 00:00:25.360
and critical thinking that proves&nbsp;
indispensable in the workplace.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.360 --> 00:00:29.840
At one point, he brings his spud gun into&nbsp;
work in a duffel bag, just to test the office&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.840 --> 00:00:34.240
park's security. Who else would take that&nbsp;
initiative? Plus, he's all about loyalty.

00:00:35.360 --> 00:00:38.640
"But, if there were somewhere else&nbsp;
that valued that loyalty more highly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:42.720
I'm going wherever they value loyalty the most."

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:45.360
Don't think about that one too hard.

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:49.760
By all accounts - particularly his own - Dwight&nbsp;
is a force to be reckoned with and an asset to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.760 --> 00:00:54.800
be paid attention to. But the people around Dwight&nbsp;
don't regard him this way very often — or at all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.800 --> 00:00:59.920
really. Dwight isn't actually treated fairly&nbsp;
in most instances. His boss and idol Michael&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.920 --> 00:01:05.360
Scott repeatedly spurns him, both in terms of&nbsp;
friendship and the promotions Dwight craves.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.360 --> 00:01:09.680
Consider Michael's Rolodex entry on&nbsp;
Dwight, which reads simply, quote:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:15.360
"Tall. Beets." Might Dwight, the resident weirdo&nbsp;
of "The Office," be its underappreciated hero?

00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:18.800
There are many signs that point to&nbsp;
this being true, but something that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.800 --> 00:01:23.280
happens during "The Office" Season 2 really&nbsp;
drives this point home. Here's the "Office"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:26.640
conflict that has fans reevaluating&nbsp;
[RE-aval-u-a-ting] Dwight - and Jim.

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:30.480
Of course, nobody wants to dislike the&nbsp;
big hero Jim. And it is entertaining&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.480 --> 00:01:33.760
to watch Dwight swing wildly&nbsp;
between oblivious incompetence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.760 --> 00:01:38.000
frustration, and genuine dominance where&nbsp;
he uses his intelligence to command a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:42.640
situation and the people in it. Over time,&nbsp;
however, it's hard not to notice how often&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.640 --> 00:01:47.680
Dwight is met with dismissiveness and even&nbsp;
full-on antagonism no matter how he behaves.

00:01:47.680 --> 00:01:51.360
That's not to say he doesn't&nbsp;
sometimes instigate problems himself.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:55.280
The incessant prank war between Dwight&nbsp;
and Jim Halpert, the time Dwight put the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.280 --> 00:01:59.520
entire office in real danger to prove a point&nbsp;
about being prepared in the event of a fire,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.520 --> 00:02:03.920
the verbal jabs he throws at his fellow Dunder&nbsp;
Mifflin employees — that's just the tip of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.920 --> 00:02:09.120
iceberg. But the issue is that Dwight isn't always&nbsp;
to blame, and when he feels uncomfortable at work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.120 --> 00:02:14.400
and turns to his company for help, he is,&nbsp;
at best, laughed at. At worst, he's ignored.

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:18.160
One of the worst examples of the lack&nbsp;
of respect Dwight gets comes from human&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.160 --> 00:02:23.520
resources representative Toby Flenderson in&nbsp;
the Season 2 episode "Conflict Resolution."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.520 --> 00:02:28.720
The episode sees Dwight approaching Toby with&nbsp;
some very legitimate claims about Jim's behavior.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.720 --> 00:02:32.640
It's Toby's job to hear Dwight out,&nbsp;
address his concerns appropriately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.640 --> 00:02:37.440
and resolve the conflict between Dwight&nbsp;
and Jim. Unfortunately, Toby neglects him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.440 --> 00:02:41.520
sticking all of Dwight's complaints in&nbsp;
a box rather than taking them seriously.

00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:45.600
"The Office" is a comedy, of course, but if&nbsp;
we're thinking in terms of real-life companies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:49.040
Toby's actions are almost&nbsp;
certainly a violation of procedure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.040 --> 00:02:52.640
and outside of a professional&nbsp;
context, it's just a jerk move.

00:02:52.640 --> 00:02:56.880
As many fans have pointed out, Toby kind&nbsp;
of lets everyone down in the episode,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.880 --> 00:03:01.040
as it reveals that he's kept a massive file&nbsp;
of unresolved complaints from Dunder Mifflin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.040 --> 00:03:05.680
employees held back. But it's particularly hard&nbsp;
on Dwight, who's clearly complained about Jim&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.680 --> 00:03:11.360
for years and been ignored basically every time.&nbsp;
Sure, Dwight's a lot to handle on his own, but if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.360 --> 00:03:16.720
an employee is that concerned about a coworker,&nbsp;
ignoring it probably isn't the best response.

00:03:16.720 --> 00:03:21.760
Dwight is visibly distressed by Jim's actions.&nbsp;
He doesn't find Jim's antics funny, and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.760 --> 00:03:26.800
show doesn't depict their prank war as harmless&nbsp;
fun between friends. Jim's behavior messes with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.800 --> 00:03:31.920
Dwight's sense of his own sanity, his emotions,&nbsp;
and his ability to do his job. At one point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.920 --> 00:03:36.000
Jim even tricks him into going into the&nbsp;
women's restroom. This adds up to a textbook&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:41.360
hostile work environment, but Toby never lifts&nbsp;
a finger to do what he is literally paid to do.

00:03:41.360 --> 00:03:45.520
Fans of "The Office" are now looking back on&nbsp;
the episode and feeling sympathy for Dwight,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.520 --> 00:03:49.120
seeing him as a complicated character&nbsp;
who deserved some kindness here.

